// @expect-error Function 'usesReturn' is 'noreturn', so it never returns and cannot contain 'return'. Throw, call 'panic' or call another 'noreturn' function instead.
// @expect-error Function 'fallsOffEnd' is declared 'noreturn', but a path through its body ends normally. End every path by throwing, calling 'panic' or calling another 'noreturn' function.
// @expect-error A 'noreturn' function never returns a value, so it declares no return type. Remove the '-> ...' clause after the parameter list.
// @expect-error The constructor of 'Widget' cannot be marked 'noreturn'. A constructor always returns the new object. Remove 'noreturn'.
// @expect-error The destructor of 'Widget' cannot be marked 'noreturn'. A destructor runs when the object is destroyed, and it always returns. Remove 'noreturn'.
// @expect-error Override of 'boom' must be marked 'noreturn', because the 'boom' it overrides in 'Base' is. Add 'noreturn' before the method name.
// @expect-error Function 'viaSafeCall' returns 'int', but a path through its body ends without a 'return'. Add a 'return' at the end of every path.
// @expect-error build failed with 7 problems
// Error cases for the `noreturn` modifier: a `return` statement, a body that can fall off the
// end, a return-type clause, `noreturn` on a constructor and on a destructor, an override that
// drops `noreturn`, and a safe call to a `noreturn` method, which does not end a path.

noreturn usesReturn() {
    return;
}

noreturn fallsOffEnd() {
    print("this can complete normally");
}

noreturn withReturnType() -> int {
    panic("x");
}

class Widget {
    noreturn constructor() {
        panic("x");
    }

    noreturn destructor() {
        panic("x");
    }
}

abstract class Base {
    abstract noreturn boom();
}

class Derived extends Base {
    override boom() {
        panic("dropped the noreturn contract");
    }
}

// A safe call runs nothing when the object is null, so it does not end the path.
viaSafeCall(Base? base) -> int {
    base?.boom();
}
